earlier this month the u.s ambassador to  the united nations linda thomas  greenfield  visited the last border crossing  bringing humanitarian aid from turkey  into syria  babal hawa is according to the un a  lifeline for 2.4 million people  but its fate now hangs in the balance in  2014 the un security council  authorized four border crossings but the  others didn't stay open after russia and  china used their veto power  two council members have now put forward  a draft resolution  which would again allow two crossing  points so what nori and ireland did was  without looking at anything other than  the needs  we proposed a renewal and an expansion  of the mandate  but at a news conference the russian  ambassador made it pretty clear  he'd be prepared to use his veto to stop  a second border point  we told him in the very beginning that  what we hear from our colleagues about  reopening the closed cross-border points  is really a non-stutter  we are discussing uh the one that is  remaining  are you saying you currently do not see  a need for even a single  border crossing i will not give you any  definite  definitive answer at this time i will  only say that we are continuing we  continue consulting on that issue  germany's outgoing un ambassador who led  negotiations on the issue when his  country was on the security council  told al jazeera other countries must  stand strong  we cannot allow and agree to a solution  where all of a sudden humanitarian aid  is cut for  you know some russian ambition to  support the regime of assad the issue  was discussed earlier this month between  president putin and president biden  and has been the subject of recent talks  between the russian and turkish foreign  ministers  so will the last border crossing into  syria close  the un security council is due to vote  before the 10th of july deadline  james bayes al jazeera at the united  nations
 

